WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.480
We were really
trying to increase

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.010
the efficiency by which a
report is generated from what

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:28.160
surgical pathologists do on a
day to day basis, which is look

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.820
at tissue sections under a
microscope and render diagnoses

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.215
after examination and studies.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.610
We're four students at MIT--

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:45.450
MBA, EMBA, and then
two PhD students.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.000
And we've been
working on a project

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.940
to rethink the
process of sign out

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.580
in the pathology
department, and facilitate

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.760
the transition to a paperless
diagnosis entry system.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.970
We've been thinking
about overall workflows--

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.150
kind of who's doing
what during the day,

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.740
to the granularity of how the
monitor is set up in the booth.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.040
We did a lot of observation from
the transcriptionists at 6:30

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.740
in the morning, to
the slide technicians

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.800
and the gross description
at 7 o'clock at night.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.050
And really seeing that
full workflow of what's

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.410
happening every day.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:24.540
Do you feel like overall
typing versus handwriting

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.310
when you're doing
slide previewing,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.070
do you think it's
better or worse?

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.410
I think it's actually
much easier to type it.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.080
By having the H Lab
come in and work

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.125
with us having a fresh set
of eyes from the outside,

00:01:37.125 --> 00:01:38.000
because we're biased.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.780
We're in the system,
we're biased.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.640
It's great to have
that set of eyes

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.600
because they will say, well,
yes, if we change one thing,

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.260
it could change
four other things.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.730
So we're trying to
anticipate what might happen.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.480
I talked with one of the
members of the staff.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.760
She told me this
experience would be better

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.783
for the residents if
we had two screens.

00:02:05.783 --> 00:02:07.200
And I was looking
at the office, I

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.158
mean, there's some
obviously that you clearly--

00:02:09.158 --> 00:02:13.740
the way they're set up, you can
not put easily a second screen.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.150
We are proposing a change.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.890
And we're proposing a
change that will impact all

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.570
the doctors', basically,
day to day life.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.200
And there are some people
who've been working here

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.280
for 30 years in their own way.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.190
And they have already
figured out the best,

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.830
kind of the most
efficient way for them.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.440
And we want to bring
technology to make

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:39.210
their life a lot more efficient,
but suggesting this change

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:43.260
and expecting this change
to be accepted by doctors

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.954
who are used to their own way
of doing, is very challenging.

00:02:51.752 --> 00:02:54.210
One of the themes that we've
really seen from our interview

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:57.840
so far is there's a fear of
the documentation burden being

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.310
shifted to
physicians, and that's

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.710
something that we need to--

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.840
Change is, of course,
difficult, and people

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.900
have a lot of anxiety
about some of the changes

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.490
we're talking about in terms
of paperless implementation.

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:12.480
We're putting out a
survey to the staff--

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.730
how many minutes a day do you
spend with just IT issues?

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:18.985
We can implement all of this.

00:03:18.985 --> 00:03:20.610
And if we don't have
the infrastructure

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.649
to support what we're trying
to do, then it's meaningless.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:37.650
We have observed-- and
we'll be very honest

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.140
here with the frustrations
and skepticism

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.180
around paperless
initiatives-- when

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.220
we ask different
doctors like, oh, how

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.860
do you go about
asking for new devices

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.020
when the systems
are very antiquated?

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.080
Almost all the doctors had
very different responses.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:53.640
And that was very
surprising to me

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.490
because, in fact,
in any enterprise,

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.620
IT support systems should be--

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.410
everyone should have a single
kind of email or hotline

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:02.747
that they can reach out to.

00:04:02.747 --> 00:04:05.080
One of the frustrations that
a lot of pathologists have,

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.770
including myself at times, is
when you go to partner's IT,

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:09.600
you get a ticket,
you wait, they come,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:10.950
and they rebuild your computer.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.180
That's their solution.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.980
If we are really pushing that
we move to a paperless system,

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:23.370
this IT support system can
not provide a good foundation

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:26.730
to allow our doctors to be
successful in this system.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:37.770
The student team built
a simulation model

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:41.770
to then show the leadership
here the different changes

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:45.000
in what the effects
would be and where

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.060
they should start from an
implementation standpoint.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.700
In the simulation model,
the way this works is we

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.160
have cases arriving
according to a process that's

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.270
based on the data.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.940
You go from accessioning
to gross examination,

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:59.940
then the case splits for slide
processing and collation,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.470
and continues through.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.360
As it gets to certain
steps, and there

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.567
are no resources available,
it ends up waiting in a queue.

00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:09.150
There are different ways you can
choose to allocate resources.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:10.740
Maybe changing the
shift schedule,

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.940
changing the way that you
staff the slide technician

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:16.920
at certain times
versus transcriptionist

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.200
or how long can you
let the QA review

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.330
go before it becomes
a bottleneck?

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.250
Types of questions
like that, we think,

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.900
could be answered
with this model.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:27.990
We knew where we
wanted to get, we just

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.920
didn't know how to get there.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:31.860
They pointed out the
challenges, the things

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:35.400
we need to do to help
overcome those obstacles,

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:39.960
and then gave us phases in
which to implement this program.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:43.290
Using these tools, they
now have the data in order

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:47.880
to affect change within
their own organization.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.000
So it gives them credibility
amongst their peers,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.850
and with their leadership, in
order to improve the system.

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:56.940
The final product
is really amazing.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.070
It's something that you
can do something with.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:00.880
There's a set of
practical solutions.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.660
There's a proposed timeline
for implementation.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.580
The project itself
actually taught you

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.620
how to interact with
the organization.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:11.220
Get your hands dirty and see
how things are working on a day

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:12.325
to day basis.

00:06:12.325 --> 00:06:13.950
At the end of the
class, you definitely

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:16.620
realize the system is as
complex, and probably more

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.200
complex, than you thought
it was when you went in,

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.030
but you also see
that there really

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:23.800
are so many opportunities
to improve the system.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.650
And there are so many
people from industry,

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:30.510
from academic settings, who
are really committed to making

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:31.330
those change.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:33.720
So there's no shortage
of ideas, and there's

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.250
no shortage to
buy-in, and it's just

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:36.970
a matter of getting it done.

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:38.700
I learned many things
from my colleagues.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.970
I learned from my teachers,
I learned from my hosts,

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.740
I learned from everybody.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.070
And I hope that others have also
learned something from myself.

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:50.370
So this exchange of knowledge,
exchange of experience

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:55.350
is a journey that we really see
ourselves growing over time,

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:59.270
as people, as professionals,
as human beings.

